1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:04,898
I'm [Name];
I led data quality and preparation.

2
00:00:04,898 --> 00:00:06,736
I profiled before cleaning.

3
00:00:06,736 --> 00:00:13,011
NHANES stores 'refused' and 'don't know'
as numeric codes such as 7, 9, 77 and 99.

4
00:00:13,011 --> 00:00:16,648
The common shortcut sets those values
to missing in every column.

5
00:00:16,648 --> 00:00:22,523
That catches 57 genuine codes,
and destroys 1,496 valid measurements,

6
00:00:22,523 --> 00:00:26,700
including HbA1c
values of exactly 7.0 percent.

7
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:32,480
Each 7.0 is above the 6.5 threshold:
a diabetes case.

8
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:37,435
The shortcut erased 29 of 721
HbA1c-based positives.

9
00:00:38,685 --> 00:00:40,145
Only six changed label;

10
00:00:40,145 --> 00:00:44,009
the rest had also self-reported,
so the either-or label hid the damage.

11
00:00:44,009 --> 00:00:45,900
No summary statistic shows this.

12
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:47,531
Second, outliers.

13
00:00:47,531 --> 00:00:52,278
The 1.5 times IQR rule
flags 542 HbA1c values.

14
00:00:53,328 --> 00:00:55,691
Its upper fence lies above
the diagnostic threshold,

15
00:00:55,691 --> 00:01:01,138
so the 519 high values are diabetes
by definition: the disease, not errors.

16
00:01:01,138 --> 00:01:02,900
We reviewed and kept them all.

17
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:07,202
[Name]'s exploration
found redundancy and skew.

18
00:01:07,202 --> 00:01:10,158
Weight and BMI show
severe multicollinearity:

19
00:01:10,158 --> 00:01:14,203
variance inflation factors
of 95.7 and 97.6.

20
00:01:15,253 --> 00:01:19,244
Fasting triglycerides are strongly
right-skewed: skewness 6.62.

21
00:01:20,294 --> 00:01:22,038
Both matter for a linear model,

22
00:01:22,038 --> 00:01:25,906
so the systematic preparation drops weight
and applies a Yeo-Johnson transform,

23
00:01:25,906 --> 00:01:27,900
which brings the skewness close to zero.

24
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:31,099
Each finding became a switchable step,

25
00:01:31,099 --> 00:01:36,052
fourteen in all, each tied
to its evidence, and none reads the label.

26
00:01:37,102 --> 00:01:40,416
From them we built three
versions of the data.

27
00:01:40,416 --> 00:01:42,671
Naive keeps every column.

28
00:01:42,671 --> 00:01:48,684
Minimal adds one rule: drop items
that exist only after a diagnosis,

29
00:01:48,684 --> 00:01:50,143
such as treatment.

30
00:01:50,143 --> 00:01:52,677
Systematic adds my other steps,

31
00:01:52,677 --> 00:01:56,786
from per-variable recoding
to transforms and class weights.

32
00:01:56,786 --> 00:02:00,100
All three feed the same model
on the same folds.

33
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:02,966
Most unexpected for me:

34
00:02:02,966 --> 00:02:07,603
one routine cleaning line erased
29 diabetic HbA1c results,

35
00:02:07,603 --> 00:02:08,924
and the label hid it.

36
00:02:10,174 --> 00:02:12,720
But cleaning cannot fix the label itself.

37
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:13,900
[Name] shows why.
